@page "/components/ai/copybutton"

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/copybutton"
            Title="AiCopyButton"
            Description="BitAiCopyButton copies a text to the clipboard and confirms it: the glyph turns into a check mark, the name of the button says so, and the copy is announced to screen readers." />

<DemoPage Name="AiCopyButton"
          SecondaryNames="@(["CopyToClipboard"])"
          Description="BitAiCopyButton copies a text to the clipboard and confirms it, because a copy button that gives no feedback leaves the user guessing whether it worked: for a moment after the copy its glyph is a check mark, its name says Copied, and a polite live region announces it. It is an icon button by default, with its label beside the icon on request, and is disabled while it has no Content."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          GitHubAIUrl="CopyButton/BitAiCopyButton.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/CopyButton/BitAiCopyButtonDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            The button needs no service. It writes through the Clipboard API of the browser, which only works in a secure context
            (https, or localhost); elsewhere, and where the browser refuses it, it falls back to the copy command of the page, and
            it only confirms a copy that happened. Its texts (Copy, Copied) come from the strings in force, which a
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/optionsprovider">BitAiOptionsProvider</BitLink> localizes. The code blocks and the
            message actions of a chat carry one; see the <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/basic">basic scenario</BitLink>.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                Give it the <b>Content</b> to copy. Press it: for two seconds the glyph is a check mark and the name of the button
                says Copied, and a screen reader hears it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem">
                <code>dotnet add package Bit.BlazorUI.AI</code>
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="dotnet add package Bit.BlazorUI.AI" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Label & ShowLabel" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" Id="example2">
            <div>
                <b>Label</b> renames the button - its tooltip and its accessible name - and <b>ShowLabel</b> shows the name beside the
                icon, Copied included while the confirmation lasts.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:1rem">
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="https://blazorui.bitplatform.dev" Label="Copy link" />
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="https://blazorui.bitplatform.dev" ShowLabel />
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="https://blazorui.bitplatform.dev" Label="Copy link" ShowLabel />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="ConfirmationDuration & OnCopied" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                <b>ConfirmationDuration</b> is how long the confirmation stays on the button (two seconds by default); a copy while it
                is showing starts it over. <b>OnCopied</b> is raised once the text is on the clipboard - never for a copy the browser
                refused.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem">
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="An answer worth keeping."
                                 ShowLabel
                                 ConfirmationDuration="TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)"
                                 OnCopied="HandleCopied" />
                <span>Copied @copyCount time(s)</span>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Content" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                <b>Content</b> is read when the button is pressed, so it can follow what changes - here what is typed. While it is
                null the button is disabled: here until something is typed, and again once the field is cleared, which sets the
                bound value back to null. An empty text that is given as such (<b>Content=""</b>) is still copied.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem">
                <BitTextField @bind-Value="draft" Immediate Placeholder="Type something to copy" Style="flex:1" />
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="@draft" />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Disabled" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                A <b>Disabled</b> copy button copies nothing, whoever presses it - the click a screen reader or a script still
                delivers to a disabled button included - and raises no <b>OnCopied</b>. A button with no <b>Content</b> is disabled
                the same way.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem">
                <BitAiCopyButton Disabled Content="Nothing to see here" />
                <BitAiCopyButton Disabled Content="Nothing to see here" ShowLabel />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiCopyButtonParams</b> sets <b>ShowLabel</b>, <b>ConfirmationDuration</b> and the look of
                every copy button inside it; a parameter of the button itself still wins (the third hides its label).
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitParams Parameters="copyButtonParams">
                <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:1rem">
                    <BitAiCopyButton Content="First snippet" />
                    <BitAiCopyButton Content="Second snippet" />
                    <BitAiCopyButton Content="Third snippet" ShowLabel="false" />
                </div>
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> its button (BitButtonClassStyles) and its
                visually hidden live region. The copy button has no CSS variables of its own: the button is a neutral BitButton of
                the Text variant, restyled through the public <b>--bit-Button-*</b> variables, from <b>:root</b>, an ancestor or
                <b>Style</b>.
            </div>
            <br />
            @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:1rem">
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="Styled" Style="border:1px dashed var(--bit-clr-brd-pri);border-radius:0.5rem" />
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="Classy" Class="custom-class" />
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="Styles" ShowLabel Styles="@(new() { Button = new() { Root = "border-radius:2rem", Icon = "color:var(--bit-clr-pri)" } })" />
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="Classes" ShowLabel Classes="@(new() { Button = new() { Root = "custom-button" } })" />
                <span style="@cssVariablesStyle">
                    <BitAiCopyButton Content="Variables" ShowLabel />
                </span>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" Id="example8">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the label of the button stands at the left of its icon, whether the direction comes from
                <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiCopyButton Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Content="سلام دنیا" Label="کپی" ShowLabel />
            <br /><br />
            <div dir="rtl">
                <BitAiCopyButton Content="سلام دنیا" Label="کپی متن" ShowLabel />
            </div>
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
